  "query_preview": "..\nSunday through Wednesday: Dry and breezy conditions continue today \nthrough Wednesday as the overall pattern remains steady. \nTemperatures will remain near normal to slightly below normal in the \n80s to low 90s. Tuesday looks to have the best potential for locally \ncritical fire weather conditions on the Waterville Plateau, Upper \nColumbia Basin, and the southwestern portion of the Colville \nReservation. A shortwave trough will brush through Vancouver Island \nand eject northeast towards central Alberta. A dry cold front will \nbrush through central Washington and far northeast Washington \nTuesday afternoon and evening bringing west/southwest winds around \n10-20 mph with gusts up to 30 mph with relative humidities around 20-\n30 percent. A Fire Weather Watch was considered for portions of \ncentral Washington Tuesday but confidence was too low in relative \nhumidity and wind speeds hitting critical levels. There is a small \nchance (15%) of a shower at the Cascade crest Tuesday but no rain \nexpected elsewhere. A more robust shortwave trough Thursday will \nmigrate through northwest Oregon through southeast Washington \nbringing a 5-15% chance of thunderstorms to southeast Washington and \nthe southern Idaho Panhandle in the afternoon and evening. \n\nThursday through Sunday: Temperatures Thursday and Friday will \nbounce back above normal in the high 80s to high 90s as high \npressure strengthens in the desert southwest. Changes come Saturday \nas a dry cold front passage is set to occur through the day. \nDeterministic GFS and ECMWF guidance this morning have an upper \nlevel jet actually slightly stronger than yesterday around 110-120\nkt. All of the ensemble clusters indicate at least a 95th \npercentile upper level jet moving through the region. There is a\n40-80% chance of winds gusting 40+ MPH through the Columbia\nBasin and Waterville Plateau. This period will be very closely \nwatched over the next several days for elevated to critical fire\nweather conditions. There is still decent ensemble spread for\nthe pressure gradients across the region. Next Sunday looks \ncooler after the front passage with temperatures in the upper \n70s to upper 80s. /Butler",
